From: Kernel Investigation <kinv@fb.com>
Date: Tue, 30 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] btrfs: bound inode xarray lock hold time in extent map shrinker

The extent map shrinker walks a root's in-memory inodes via
find_first_inode_to_shrink(), which takes the root's inode xarray lock
(root->inodes.xa_lock) and then loops calling xa_find(), skipping every
inode that has no loaded extent maps (files opened only to stat(2) them,
directories, inodes whose extent maps were already released, etc.) until
it finds one worth scanning.

The whole skip loop runs with the xarray lock held and only calls
cond_resched_lock() between iterations. That same lock is taken by every
inode creation (btrfs_add_inode_to_root() -> xa_store()) and by every
inode eviction (btrfs_del_inode_from_root()) for the root, so holding it
across a long run of skipped inodes starves those tasks.

cond_resched_lock() does not bound the hold time:

  - On non-preemptible kernels (PREEMPT_NONE) spin_needbreak() always
    returns 0, so cond_resched_lock() only drops the lock when the
    current task needs rescheduling. The shrinker runs in a cpu-bound
    workqueue worker that can be the only runnable task on its CPU for a
    long time, so need_resched() may not be set for many seconds.

  - On virtualized guests without paravirt spinlocks the qspinlock falls
    back to an unfair test-and-set spinlock (virt_spin_lock()); its
    waiters are not reflected in spin_is_contended(), so even a
    preemptible kernel would not break the lock on contention.

The result observed in production (a 192-CPU KVM guest, PREEMPT_NONE) is
the extent map shrinker holding root->inodes.xa_lock while scanning a
root with a very large, sparse inode set, while ~25 other CPUs spin in
queued_spin_lock_slowpath()/virt_spin_lock() trying to create or evict
inodes on the same root, producing a soft lockup:

  watchdog: BUG: soft lockup - CPU#156 stuck for 22s! [chef-client]
   queued_spin_lock_slowpath+0x22/0x290
   xa_store+0x17/0x40
   btrfs_add_inode_to_root      (inlined)
   btrfs_create_new_inode+0x5b9/0xc50
   btrfs_create_common+0x181/0x220
   path_openat / do_filp_open / __x64_sys_openat

Bound the lock hold time: in addition to cond_resched_lock(),
unconditionally drop and reacquire the lock every
BTRFS_EM_SHRINK_LOCK_BATCH skipped inodes. Because 'from' is an index
(not a dereferenced pointer) and xa_find() re-finds the next present
entry >= from after reacquiring, correctness is preserved; only the
lock-hold granularity changes. Dropping the (unfair) lock repeatedly
gives the waiting create/evict tasks windows to acquire it, eliminating
the multi-second stalls.

Signed-off-by: Kernel Investigation <kinv@fb.com>
---
 fs/btrfs/extent_map.c | 42 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 41 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/fs/btrfs/extent_map.c b/fs/btrfs/extent_map.c
index 02bfdb976e40..d3d7ba237e70 100644
--- a/fs/btrfs/extent_map.c
+++ b/fs/btrfs/extent_map.c
@@ -1191,11 +1191,19 @@ static long btrfs_scan_inode(struct btrfs_inode *inode, struct btrfs_em_shrink_c
 	return nr_dropped;
 }

+/*
+ * Number of inodes to skip while holding the root's inode xarray lock in
+ * find_first_inode_to_shrink() before unconditionally releasing it so that
+ * other tasks contending on the lock can make progress.
+ */
+#define BTRFS_EM_SHRINK_LOCK_BATCH 100
+
 static struct btrfs_inode *find_first_inode_to_shrink(struct btrfs_root *root,
 						      u64 min_ino)
 {
 	struct btrfs_inode *inode;
 	unsigned long from = min_ino;
+	unsigned int skipped = 0;

 	xa_lock(&root->inodes);
 	while (true) {
@@ -1234,7 +1242,39 @@ static struct btrfs_inode *find_first_inode_to_shrink(struct btrfs_root *root,
 		write_unlock(&tree->lock);
 next:
 		from = btrfs_ino(inode) + 1;
-		cond_resched_lock(&root->inodes.xa_lock);
+		/*
+		 * A root can have a huge number of inodes cached in memory that
+		 * have no loaded extent maps (files opened only to stat(2) them,
+		 * directories, inodes whose extent maps were already released,
+		 * etc.), and we skip all of them here while holding the inode
+		 * xarray lock. That same lock is taken by every inode creation
+		 * (btrfs_add_inode_to_root()) and eviction
+		 * (btrfs_del_inode_from_root()) for this root, so holding it
+		 * while scanning a long run of such inodes starves those tasks.
+		 *
+		 * cond_resched_lock() is not enough to bound the hold time: on
+		 * non-preemptible kernels spin_needbreak() is always 0, so the
+		 * lock is only dropped when the current task needs rescheduling,
+		 * which may not happen for a long time for a cpu-bound worker
+		 * that is the only runnable task on its CPU. On virtualized
+		 * guests, which fall back to an unfair test-and-set spinlock,
+		 * the contention is not even detectable via spin_is_contended().
+		 * The result is that the extent map shrinker can hold this lock
+		 * for many seconds, causing soft lockups in tasks creating or
+		 * evicting inodes on the same root.
+		 *
+		 * So, in addition to cond_resched_lock(), unconditionally drop
+		 * and reacquire the lock every BTRFS_EM_SHRINK_LOCK_BATCH skipped
+		 * inodes to give waiters a chance to grab it and make progress.
+		 */
+		if (++skipped >= BTRFS_EM_SHRINK_LOCK_BATCH) {
+			skipped = 0;
+			xa_unlock(&root->inodes);
+			cond_resched();
+			xa_lock(&root->inodes);
+		} else {
+			cond_resched_lock(&root->inodes.xa_lock);
+		}
 	}
 	xa_unlock(&root->inodes);

